    {
      "id": "a02",
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "score": 3,
      "estimated_minutes": 3,
      "reason": "One additional look at NOA §11.1.3 verifies the quoted reference to applicable federal law. The remaining comparison is clear-cut: the asserted choice-of-law conflict concerns different words and a different problem from d02’s heading-versus-substance dispute."
    },
    {
      "id": "a03",
      "difficulty": "intermediate",
      "score": 5,
      "estimated_minutes": 8,
      "reason": "Verification takes work because the description asserts that mediation conditions both arbitration and litigation and that neither document supplies a mediation obligation or procedure. The reviewer must check §4.6’s sentence structure and the two documents’ procedures; once checked, the mediation-trigger complaint is readily distinguished from d03’s residual-incorporation issue."
    },
    {
      "id": "a04",
      "difficulty": "hard",
      "score": 7,
      "estimated_minutes": 15,
      "reason": "The reviewer must test the claimed clash between mandatory NOA arbitration and the Guaranty’s litigation default, then determine how much of d03 that complaint identifies. The description reaches the conflicting procedures and incorporation sentence but omits continued performance, requiring a judgment about whether it captures the court’s carve-out construction or only the surrounding procedural conflict."
    }
  ]
}
